the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a sign* is a sign.
    *a sign* beats *a second sign*.
    the winner of *a game* is *a player*.
    *a game* is a game; scenario element.
    *a player* is a player; scenario element.
    *a player* played in *a game*; scenario element.
    *a player* threw *a sign* in *a game*; scenario element.

the knowledge base rps includes:

the text of the Rock Paper Scissors Act is at "sources/rps.md".

the text of the Blawx project is at "sources/rps.yaml".

% The Act, section by section.
rock is a sign, as stated in the Rock Paper Scissors Act at section 2 a.

paper is a sign, as stated in the Rock Paper Scissors Act at section 2 b.

scissors is a sign, as stated in the Rock Paper Scissors Act at section 2 c.

rock beats scissors, as stated in the Rock Paper Scissors Act at section 3 a.

paper beats rock, as stated in the Rock Paper Scissors Act at section 3 b.

scissors beats paper, as stated in the Rock Paper Scissors Act at section 3 c.

rule section_4 with provenance the Rock Paper Scissors Act at section 4:
the winner of a game is a player if
    the game is a game
    and the player is a player
    and a second player is a player
    and the player is not equal to the second player
    and the player played in the game
    and the second player played in the game
    and the player threw a sign in the game
    and the second player threw a second sign in the game
    and the sign beats the second sign.

scenario bobjane is, as stated in the Blawx project at test bobjane:
    testgame is a game.
    bob is a player.
    jane is a player.
    bob played in testgame.
    jane played in testgame.
    jane threw rock in testgame.
    bob threw scissors in testgame.
    bobjane expects answers ["the winner of testgame is jane"].

scenario hypothetical is, as stated in the Blawx project at test hypothetical:
    % Blawx cannot hypothesize across disunity blocks (_ and _
    % are not the same object). Because that block is used in
    % our section 4 encoding, we need to be explicit about who
    % the players are, but Blawx can hypothesize about the rest.
    % We ask specifically whether bob won any games so that we don't
    % get the same three answers twice, once for each possible player.
    bob is a player.
    jane is a player.
    % pending — abductive: the test lets s(CASP) assume game/1, player/2, throw/3 (#abducible); Blawx's answers are hypotheses, which an LE scenario states as facts or not at all:
    % hypothetical expects answers ["the winner of a game is bob"].

scenario who_wins is, as stated in the Blawx project at test who_wins:
    who_wins expects answers [].

scenario case_1 is, as stated in the case analysis of the encoding at section 4:
    % a case of the rule of section 4 (blawx_cases.pl), answered by Blawx
    game_a is a game.
    player_a is a player.
    player_b is a player.
    player_a played in game_a.
    player_b played in game_a.
    player_a threw sign_a in game_a.
    player_b threw sign_b in game_a.
    sign_a beats sign_b.
    which_the_winner_of_is expects answers ["the winner of game_a is player_a"].

